INT. BOOKBINDERY - SUNDAY AFTERNOON

Quiet. Presses stand like iron monks. Spools of linen thread, bone folders, glue pots. Sun slants through dusty skylights.

LIORA MENDELSOHN (30s) trims a spine with a knife. MATEUS FARIA (30s) presses a stack of pages under a brass weight. SEVERINE ACHARD (50s), the owner, sorts paper by tone. ODELL BRIGHT (20s) sweeps.

                    LIORA
          Mateus, hand me the paste.

                    MATEUS
          Which paste?

                    LIORA
          The wheat.

                    MATEUS
          There are two wheats.

                    SEVERINE
          The older one, dear. It's more patient.

Mateus passes her a pot. Their fingers touch. Both glance away.

                    ODELL
          Should I do the back room?

                    SEVERINE
          You did it Friday.

                    ODELL
          It got dusty.

                    SEVERINE
          It's a bookbindery.

                    LIORA
          Odell, come hold this.

                    MATEUS
          Odell, come hold this.

                    LIORA
          ^I asked first.

                    MATEUS
          ^I only asked second because you were slow.

Odell, caught between them, stands with his broom like a flagpole.

                    ODELL
          I'll hold his.

                    LIORA
          Traitor.

                    SEVERINE
          Children. The glue is setting.

All four go quiet and return to their benches. Scissors snip. Paper whispers. Outside a church bell rings four.

                    MATEUS
          (low, to Liora)
          Dinner?

                    LIORA
          (low)
          Maybe.

                    SEVERINE
          I heard that.

                    LIORA
          You weren't meant to.

                    SEVERINE
          Everything in this room was meant to be heard. It's a very small room.
